title: File tracking
kind: overview
aliases: tracking track watch untracked
summary: Tracking is opt-in — nothing is followed until mf_watch is set — and a tracked file's metarecord follows it as it moves, changes and disappears.

A metarecord can describe anything, but most describe a file: they carry an
`mfr_path` (their position in the repository's tree) and the `mfr_*` fields read from
the filesystem ([[Reserved fields]]). ''Tracking'' is keeping those in step with the
disk:

* the ''watcher'' follows live events and records them in batches — a file created, changed, moved, deleted;
* ''reconcile'' ([[Reconcile]]) walks the tree on demand, creating what is missing and recognising moved files by their content;
* both obey the same ''eligibility'' rules: what is watched, what is ignored.

Nothing is tracked until you ask. A new repository has `mf_watch = false` on its root:
set it to `true` on a directory to follow that directory and everything below it
([[Tracking a folder]]). `mf_ignore` patterns then leave out build output, dependency
trees and the like ([[Ignore presets]]).

A deleted file's metarecord is kept — only its `mfr_path` becomes `nothing` — so your
own fields and its history survive. And a subtree you stop watching is deliberately
''frozen'': the daemon no longer touches its metadata ([[Freezing metadata by not watching]]).

